parts=['<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Embedding selectors for NN explanations</title><style>body{font:16px system-ui;max-width:1250px;margin:40px auto;padding:0 24px;color:#22303a}h1,h2{color:#244f65}table{border-collapse:collapse;font-size:14px;margin:20px 0}td,th{padding:7px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;text-align:right}td:first-child,th:first-child{text-align:left}img{width:100%}.note{background:#eef4f6;padding:16px;line-height:1.6}p,li{line-height:1.6}</style></head><body>', '<h1>Can embeddings improve when we show a nearest neighbour?</h1>','<p>Four toxicity datasets: Aegis, BeaverTails, ToxicChat and ToxiGen. Ten judges, five backbones, 200 test cells. Every selector and cutoff was chosen using validation only; test results below did not select any configuration.</p>', '<div class="note"><strong>Reading this comparison.</strong> Embeddings describe the target and its original training neighbour. The selector chooses between the already-collected control and NN answers; it does not retrain either judge. The matched numerical baseline uses the same 50 judge–backbone groups. The previous pooled baseline is stronger evidence of practical added value because it shares more training data across backbones.</div>', '<h2>Conclusion</h2><p><strong>Keep the numerical selector for now.</strong> Embeddings alone reach 78.465% F1 and embeddings plus numerical features 78.749%, versus 79.073% for the previous toxicity-pooled numerical selector. On the matched 50-group design, numerical-only scores 78.818%; adding embeddings changes F1 by −0.069 percentage points (conditional 95% interval −0.205 to +0.069). There is no convincing added value from embeddings in this experiment.</p><p>The combined selector improves over always showing NN by +0.439 points, but this is not an improvement over the simpler existing gate. Embeddings alone are essentially tied with the cosine gate. Under the three tuned anchors, embeddings and combined inputs also fail to improve on always showing NN or the previous numerical gate.</p><p>Effects vary by judge. Combined inputs improve most over always-NN for Mistral (+3.31 points) and OLMo (+1.57), while reducing F1 for several others. The previous toxicity-pooled selector remains better on average on each of the four datasets. One plausible limitation is training-data scarcity: the median backbone–judge selector has only 82.5 validation disagreements, despite thousands of embedding coordinates. PCA and regularization address this partially; these results do not prove that scarcity is the cause. The top validation candidates are often nearly tied, and their small validation advantages do not translate into a better test average.</p><h2>Primary test: fixed probe and judge at 50</h2><img src="embedding_comparison.png">',metric_table('Fixed 50/50'),'<h2>Paired comparisons</h2><p>Changes are F1 percentage points. Intervals use 2,000 target-cluster multiplier replicates, shared across judges/backbones and calibrated within backbone/dataset/original sampling stratum. Census strata remain fixed. These are conditional uncertainty estimates for the fitted models; they do not include model-selection uncertainty or correct for trying many families.</p>',table(pd.DataFrame(ci).query("method in ['embedding','combined','selected']")),'<h2>By judge, backbone and dataset</h2>']
for group in ['model','backbone','dataset']:
 tab=z[['always_nn','prior_pooled','tabular','embedding','combined','selected']].groupby(level=group).mean()*100;parts+=['<h3>'+group.title()+'</h3>',table(tab.reset_index())]
parts+=['<h2>Threshold robustness: three anchors averaged</h2><p>For each original control-, NN- and saliency-tuned threshold pair, all explanation conditions retain that same pair. Selector architectures transfer from fixed-50 validation; C/k and inclusion cutoffs are reselected on validation for each anchor. This is a restricted architecture-transfer check, not a fresh full architecture search per anchor.</p>',metric_table('Three anchors averaged'),'<h2>Training evidence and scope</h2>',f'<p>10,000 validation contexts and {uniquecontexts:,} retained test contexts across backbones ({uniquetargets:,} distinct test target groups); {len(fixed):,} judge–context test decisions. The supervised selector training signal comes only from validation disagreements: {int(fixedtr.training_disagreements.sum()):,} across the 50 groups, median {fixedtr.training_disagreements.median():.0f}, range {fixedtr.training_disagreements.min()}–{fixedtr.training_disagreements.max()} per group.</p>','<p>377 candidates per primary group: logistic regression with five regularization strengths; k-NN with Euclidean/cosine distances and six neighbourhood sizes; raw and probe-standardized embeddings; direct and PCA 16/64/128 coordinates; concatenation and pair interactions; numerical-feature combinations; target-only and neighbour-only PCA64 logistic ablations. PCA and scalers are fitted inside the three target-grouped validation folds. No test labels enter preprocessing, fitting or cutoff selection. Full-validation refits are evaluated once.</p>',table(choiceinfo),table(pd.DataFrame(heter)),'<h2>Limits and reproducibility</h2><p>This reuses a test set already examined in earlier project analyses, so it is an exploratory comparison with validation-selected settings, not a fresh confirmation experiment. Broad candidate selection with few disagreement labels can be unstable. A positive result here does not establish that embeddings understand the mechanism, and a negative result does not rule out a representation learned with substantially more training data. API rows retain their original valid paired support and weighted denominator; local rows retain full-population denominators. Equal-weight macro averages use all 200 cells.</p><p>All prior control, NN and cosine reference metrics are replayed exactly before accepting evaluation. Frozen choices, cutoff tables, training counts, candidate validation scores, prediction rows and checksum receipts are retained. No new LLM calls were made.</p><ul><li><a href="summary.csv">Overall metrics</a></li><li><a href="paired_intervals.csv">Paired intervals</a></li><li><a href="choices.csv">Frozen validation choices</a></li><li><a href="selected_cutoffs.csv">Selected inclusion cutoffs</a></li><li><a href="training_counts.csv">Training sample sizes</a></li><li><a href="cell_metrics.csv">All cell metrics</a></li><li><a href="embedding_comparison.pdf">Figure PDF</a></li></ul></body></html>']
